===Etymology 1===
From {{m|en|Quine}}, {{named-after|en|Willard Van Orman Quine|wplink==|nat=the American|occ=logician and philosopher|born=1908|died=2000|nocap=1}}.

Verb {{senseno|en|append}} (“to append (a text) to a quotation of itself”) was {{coinage|en|Douglas Hofstadter|nat=the American|occ=cognitive and computer scientist|nocap=1}} (born 1945) in his book ''[[w:Gödel, Escher, Bach|Gödel, Escher, Bach: An Eternal Golden Braid]]'' (1979; see the quotation), referring to Quine’s study of {{w|indirect self-referencing}} and in particular [[w:Quine's paradox|Quine’s paradox]], the following statement that produces a paradox: “‘Yields falsehood when preceded by its quotation’ yields falsehood when preceded by its quotation.” Hofstadter also referred to the concept of noun {{senseno|en|computing}} (“program that produces its own source code as output”) in the book, but termed it a ''self-rep'' rather than a quine.<ref>See, for example, {{cite-book|en|author=[[w:Douglas Hofstadter|Douglas R[ichard] Hofstadter]]|chapter=Self-ref and Self-rep|title=[[w:Gödel, Escher, Bach|Gödel, Escher, Bach: An Eternal Golden Braid]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Basic Books|year=1979|section=part II (EGB)|page=499|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/godelescherbach00doug/page/499/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-465-02685-2|passage=The preceding program is an elegant example of a self-reproducing program written in a language which was not designed to make the writing of self-reps particularly easy. {{...}} But suppose a language were designed expressly for making self-reps easy to write. {{...}} For example, suppose that the operation of eniuq-ing were a built-in feature of the language, needing no explicit definition (as we assumed PRINT was). Then a teeny self-rep would be this: / ENIUQ [‘ENIUQ’]. / It is very similar to Tortoise’s version of Quine’s version of the {{w|Epimenides}} self-ref, where the verb “to quine” is assumed to be known: / “yields falsehood when quined” yields falsehood when quined.}}</ref>

Verb {{senseno|en|philosophy}} (“to deny the importance or significance of (something obviously real or important)”) was independently {{coinage|en|Daniel Dennett|nat=the American|occ=cognitive scientist and philosopher|nocap=1}} (1942–2024) in September 1969 in the original version of his work ''{{w|The Philosophical Lexicon}}'':<ref>{{cite-book|author=w:Daniel Dennett|chapter=Preface to the Eighth Edition|mainauthor={{w|Kathleen Atkins}} [''et al.'']|editor=Daniel Dennett|title=The Philosophical Lexicon|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20240710214239/https://dl.tufts.edu/downloads/1z40m5090?filename=jw827p68f.pdf|location=Newark, Del.|publisher={{w|American Philosophical Association}}, distributor|edition=8th|year=1987|oclc=24233128|passage=The ''Lexicon'' began one night in September of 1969 when I was writing lecture notes and found myself jotting down as a heading “quining intentions”. I saw fit to compose a definition of the verb. In the morning I was ill prepared to lecture, but handed a list of about a dozen definitions together with the Introduction to my colleagues at Irvine.}}</ref> see the 1987 quotation.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|computing}}{{lb|en|computing}} A [[program#Noun|program]] that [[produce#Verb|produces]] its own [[source code]] as [[output#Noun|output]].
#* {{quote-newsgroup|en|author=John David Regehr|title=[A] Quine in C++?|newsgroup=comp.lang.misc|url=https://groups.google.com/d/msg/comp.lang.misc/-dR-TMoB3S4/CMfGrs1MzwQJ|date=10 October 1996|passage=This has been bugging me recently. Any '''quines''' or pointers to relevant articles or web pages is appreciated. Thanks!}}
#* {{quote-newsgroup|en|author=Gergo Barany|title=CC Hack?|newsgroup=comp.lang.c|url=https://groups.google.com/d/msg/comp.lang.c/EHioEFXMN_A/4BtmxcV_dlAJ|date=14 December 1999|passage=Self-reproducing programs are commonly called '''quines'''. Do a web search, it should turn up lots of them. There was also a quine thread here in comp.lang.c just days ago, search deja.com (the thread's title was something about self-printing programs, I think).}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Clinton Pierce|chapter=Advanced Perl|title=Perl Developer’s Dictionary|location=Indianapolis, Ind.|publisher=w:Sams Publishing|month=July|year=2001|page=269|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/perldevelopersdi0000pier/page/269/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-672-32067-5|passage=A '''quine''' is a program that can print its own source code. Most '''quines''' are notoriously difficult (and fiendish) to write. Perl can cheat, though. :)}}
#* {{quote-newsgroup|en|author=Arthur J. O’Dwyer|title=‘A to Z of C’|newsgroup=comp.lang.c
|url=https://groups.google.com/d/msg/comp.lang.c/mERds_qS6wg/vYrezr0rWqoJ|date=6 May 2003|passage=Why have a one-page chapter that doesn't say anything? At the least, you should present a '''quine''' program written in pure ISO C (I can send you one if you like); {{...}} you might refer the interested reader to {{w|Ken Thompson}}'s ACM lecture or to another good source of '''quine'''-related puzzles. '''Quines''' *are* a lot of fun, but why waste time with trivial ASCII-based examples when there are much more fundamental ways to create them?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:David J. Darling|David [J.] Darling]]|entry=quine|title=[[w:The Universal Book of Mathematics|The Universal Book of Mathematics: From Abracadabra to Zeno’s Paradoxes]]|location=Hoboken, N.J.|publisher=[[w:Wiley (publisher)|John Wiley & Sons]]|year=2004|page=264|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/universalbookofm0000darl/page/264/mode/1up|column=2|isbn=978-0-471-27047-8|passage=A respectable '''quine'''—one that doesn't cheat—is not allowed to do anything as underhand or trivial as seeking the source file on the disk, opening it, and copying (or printing) its contents. Although writing a '''quine''' is not always easy, and in fact may seem impossible, it can always be done in any programming language that is Turing complete (see ''Turing machine''), which includes every programming language actually in use.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Simon Cozens|chapter=Fun with Perl|editor=Allison Randal|title=Advanced Perl Programming|edition=2nd|location=Sebastopol, Calif.|publisher=[[w:O'Reilly Media|O’Reilly Media]]|year=2005|page=260|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/advancedperlprog0000coze/page/260/mode|isbn=978-0-596-00456-9|passage=SelfGOL can reproduce itself; it can turn other programs into a '''quine'''; it can display a scrolling banner; it plays the Game of Life; and it contains no (ordinary) loops, <kbd>goto</kbd> statements, or <kbd>if</kbd> statements. Control flow is done, well, interestingly.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Julian Rohrhuber|chapter=Implications of Unfolding|editors=Uwe Seifert; Jin-hyun Kim; Anthony Moore|title=Paradoxes of Interactivity: Perspectives for Media Theory, Human-computer Interaction, and Artistic Investigations|location=Bielefeld, North Rhine-Westphalia|publisher=transcript Verlag|year=2008|section=part II (Interplay between Art, Science, and Technology)|page=179|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/paradoxesofinter0000unse/page/179/mode/1up|isbn=978-3-89942-842-1|passage=Yet from a different perspective, it {{quote-gloss|the semantics of a program}} describes the process of ''producing'' this very code; in other words, it is because object- and meta-language interrelate that makes a '''quine''' difficult; in less reflective programs, where means and ends are more separate, this difficulty is not so obvious.}}
#* {{quote-newsgroup|en|author=Mike Ash|title=‘--All You Zombies--’ Title|newsgroup=rec.arts.sf.written|url=https://groups.google.com/g/rec.arts.sf.written/c/tN-qCzPjCAE/m/7Uowd0MXCmUJ|date=31 July 2009|passage=Gee, last time I wrote a '''quine''' in Lisp it ended up being kind of difficult&nbsp;…}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Antoine Amarilli [''et al.'']|chapter=Can Code Polymorphism Limit Information Leakage?|editors=Claudio Agostino Ardagna; Jianying Zhou|title=Information Security Theory and Practice: Security and Privacy of Mobile Devices in Wireless Communication{{nb...|5th IFIP WG 11.2}}|series=w:Lecture Notes in Computer Science|seriesvolume=6633|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220206180755/https://eprint.iacr.org/2011/099.pdf|location=Berlin|publisher=[[w:Springer Science+Business Media|Springer]]|year=2011|section=section 5 (Can Lisp-like Languages Help?)|page=14|isbn=978-3-642-21039-6|passage=The solution is to make a '''quine''' that is also a λ-expression (instead of a list of statements). This is possible, thanks to S-expressions. The way the '''quine''' works relies on the fact that its code is a list of statements and that the last one can take a list of the previous ones as arguments.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Thomas Meyer; Christian Tschudin|chapter=Robust Network Services with Distributed Code Rewiring|editors=Pietro Lio; w:Dinesh Verma|title=Biologically Inspired Networking and Sensing: Algorithms and Architectures|location=Hershey, Pa.|publisher=Medical Information Science Reference, IGI Global|year=2012|section=section I (New Biologically Inspired Architectures)|page=37|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/isbn_9781613500927/page/37/mode/1up|column=1|isbn=978-1-61350-092-7|passage=A '''Quine''' is a program that prints its own code. '''Quines''' exist for any programming language that is Turing complete and it is a common challenge for students to come up with a '''Quine''' in their language of choice. The '''Quine''' Page provides a comprehensive list of such programs in various languages.}}
#* {{quote-newsgroup|en|author=Brian Hodgert|title=‘Mountains will be Mountains’|newsgroup=talk.religion.buddhism|url=https://groups.google.com/d/msg/talk.religion.buddhism/Denn9TZywdo/edWL0Sat5cwJ|date=8 December 2013|passage=Upon receiving a "QUINE" request by the client, the server will first send a 01 OK response, and will then provide the client with a '''quine''' in the programming language used to implement the server. This '''quine''' does not have to be original.}}

