====Verb====
{{en-verb}} {{term-label|en|transitive}}

# {{senseid|en|append}} To [[append#Verb|append]] (a [[text#Noun|text]]) to a [[quotation]] of itself.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Douglas Hofstadter|Douglas R[ichard] Hofstadter]]|chapter=Air on G’s String|title=[[w:Gödel, Escher, Bach|Gödel, Escher, Bach: An Eternal Golden Braid]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Basic Books|year=1979|section=part II (EGB)|page=435|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/godelescherbach00doug/page/435/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-465-02685-2|passage=Anyway, now I know how to '''quine''' a phrase. It's quite amusing. Here's a '''quined''' phrase: / "IS A SENTENCE FRAGMENT" IS A SENTENCE FRAGMENT. / It's silly but all the same I enjoy it. You take a sentence fragment, '''quine''' it, and lo and behold, you've made a sentence! A true sentence, in this case.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Douglas Hofstadter|Douglas R[ichard] Hofstadter]]|chapter=Analogies and Metaphors to Explain [[w:Kurt Gödel|Gödel]]’s Theorem|editors=Douglas M. Campbell; John C. Higgins|title=Mathematics: People, Problems, Results|location=Belmont, Calif.|publisher=Wadsworth International|year=1984|page=274|isbn=978-0-534-03203-6|passage="Quining" is what I called it in my book. (He certainly didn't call it that!) '''Quining''' is an operation that I define on any string of English. {{...}} Here is an example of a '''quined''' phrase: "is a sentence with no subject" is a sentence with no subject.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=N[athaniel] S. Hellerstein|chapter=Metamathemics|title=Diamond: A Paradox Logic|series=Series on Knots and Everything|seriesvolume=14|location=Singapore|publisher=w:World Scientific|year=1997|section=part 2 (Advanced Diamond Logic)|page=183|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/diamondparadoxlo0000hell/page/183/mode/1up|isbn=978-981-022-850-7|passage=Diamond arises in Gödelian meta-mathematics. In meta-math, sentences can refer to each other’s provability, and to '''quining'''. This yields self-reference: T = “‘is provable when '''quined'''’ is provable when '''quined'''.” / D = “‘is unprovable when '''quined'''’ is unprovable when '''quined'''.” {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-newsgroup|en|author=Howard Mirowitz|title=Why is L&T in Quotation Marks?|newsgroup=rec.music.dylan|url=https://groups.google.com/d/msg/rec.music.dylan/-glZphUrLHc/ef-3ORmR7oYJ|date=1 October 2001|passage=In "[[w:Love and Theft (Bob Dylan album)|Love And Theft]]", [[w:Bob Dylan|{{quote-gloss|Bob}} Dylan]] '''quined''' the love and theft in his songs in the album's title, "Love And Theft". So the subtext, the meaning of the entire album, when preceded by its quotation, its symbol, yields a paradox.}}
# {{senseid|en|philosophy}}{{lb|en|philosophy}} To [[deny]] the [[existence]] or [[significance]] of (something [[obviously]] [[real#Adjective|real]] or [[important]]).
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Daniel Dennett|entry=quine, v.|mainauthor={{w|Kathleen Atkins}} [''et al.'']|editor=Daniel Dennett|title=The Philosophical Lexicon|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20240710214239/https://dl.tufts.edu/downloads/1z40m5090?filename=jw827p68f.pdf|location=Newark, Del.|publisher={{w|American Philosophical Association}}, distributor|edition=8th|year=1987|oclc=24233128|passage='''quine'''. v. (1) To deny resolutely the existence or importance of something real or significant. "Some philosophers have '''quined''' classes, and some have even '''quined''' physical objects." Occasionally used intr{{quote-gloss|ansitively}}, e.g., "You think I '''quine''', sir. I assure you I do not!"|brackets=on}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Howard Margolis|chapter=The Overthrow of Phlogiston: 2|title=Paradigms & Barriers: How Habits of Mind Govern Scientific Beliefs|location=Chicago, Ill.&#59; London|publisher=w:University of Chicago Press|year=1993|page=62|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/paradigmsbarrier0000marg/page/62/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-226-50522-0|passage=As with the puzzle of what happens during the combustion of a metal in pure oxygen (the "steel wool" experiment), this result can of course be '''quined'''. Taking the phlogistic view, we could say that the calx requires the same phlogiston content as the metal, so of course the amount of water absorbed must be in accord with that.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Elizabeth Pacherie|chapter=Qualia and Representations|editor=Denis Fisette|title=Consciousness and Intentionality: Models and Modalities of Attribution|series=The Western Ontario Series in Philosophy of Science|location=Dordrecht, South Holland|publisher=w:Springer Science+Business Media|year=1999|section=part 2 (Qualia and Perception)|page=119|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=yZfpCAAAQBAJ&pg=PA119|doi=10.1007/978-94-015-9193-5|isbn=978-90-481-5300-8|passage=They {{quote-gloss|some philosophers}} deny that mental states and events actually possess the qualitative properties attributed to them by qualia friends and, as a consequence, they advocate '''quining''' qualia.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Don Ross|chapter=Introduction: The Dennettian Stance|editors=Don Ross; w:Andrew Brook; David Thompson|title=Dennett’s Philosophy: A Comprehensive Assessment|series=A Bradford Book|location=Cambridge, Mass.&#59; London|publisher=w:MIT Press|year=2000|page=14|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/dennettsphilosop0000unse/page/14/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-262-18200-3|passage=Qualia are '''quined''' not because [[w:Daniel Dennett|{{quote-gloss|Daniel}} Dennett]] imagines that there is nothing it is like to be conscious, but because no clear demarcation can be drawn between representations of qualitative properties and representations of other sorts of states.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Daniel Barnett|chapter=The Private Language Machine and the Evolution of a Medium|title=Movement as Meaning: In Experimental Film|series=Consciousness, Literature & the Arts|seriesvolume=13|location=Amsterdam, North Holland&#59; New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Brill Publishers|Editions Rodopi]]|year=2008|section=part II (Dynamic and Syntactic Universals)|page=114|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=BaiUAf1dBMcC&pg=PA114|isbn=978-90-420-2385-7|issn=1573-2193|passage=One of the things that [[w:Ludwig Wittgenstein|{{quote-gloss|Ludwig}} Wittgenstein]] is most famous for is '''quining''' 'private language'. By saying that private languages can't exist Wittgenstein wanted us to recognize the inescapable function of the social fabric in language's work.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Andrew Pessin|chapter=Mental Transparency, Direct Sensation, and the Unity of the Cartesian Mind|editor=Jon Miller|title=Topics in Early Modern Philosophy of Mind|series=Studies in the History of Philosophy of Mind|seriesvolume=9|location=Dordrecht, South Holland|publisher=[[w:Springer Science+Business Media|Springer]]|year=2009|page=34|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=HDEyqVo_RdAC&pg=PA34|isbn=978-90-481-2380-3|passage=One might object that in this section I’ve not exactly '''quined''' Cartesian qualia, since my denial of the reality of phenomenal colour comes at the cost of accepting the "qualitative character" of sensory experience, with which contemporary philosophers, in fact, often identify qualia.}}

